# Discussion

The three fits differ only in how much they let one region's data speak for another,
and that single knob explains every difference in the tables above. Complete pooling
buys precision by asserting a homogeneity the data do not support; no pooling buys
honesty about heterogeneity at the cost of unusable intervals in the sparse regions;
partial pooling is the compromise that the comparison scores best.

Two limits are worth stating. The synthetic generator draws a single latency
distribution per region with no time trend, so nothing here exercises a
seasonally-varying effect. And the region count is small enough that the group-level
scale is itself weakly identified, which is exactly the regime where the prior on
that scale matters most.

